      "id": 1,
      "text": "Diehlstadt was founded by Tennessee-born merchant John Kirkpatrick, who also ran the first post office from 1876 to 1890.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diehlstadt%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village was officially platted in 1868, with its original 40‑acre layout filed on June 15, 1870.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diehlstadt%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A post office operated in Diehlstadt from 1870 until its closure in 1977, marking over a century of local mail service.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diehlstadt%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Diehlstadt sits on Missouri Route 77, just 2.5 miles north of U.S. Highway 61, a key corridor through southeast Scott County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diehlstadt%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s name combines the surname of early merchant Deal—also spelled Diehl—with the German suffix “stadt,” meaning town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diehlstadt%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Diehlstadt lies six miles southeast of the Mississippi River and nine miles northeast of Sikeston, placing it in the heart of Scott County’s rural landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
